  :adr/body "## What was missing

Claude Code and hermes both read a repository's `.claude/` and offer what they
find as `/name` -- the screenshot that started this work says so on its own
face: 「27 project skill(s) loaded from this repo」.

itonami read nothing. It had twenty-nine slash commands of its own and no way
to reach the thirty-two this workspace keeps in `.claude/skills/`.

## What is read

    .claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md   YAML front matter + a Markdown body
    .claude/commands/<name>.md       the same, without the directory

Both are Claude Code's shapes and both are read, so a repository does not have
to pick one to be usable here. The root is the nearest ancestor of the working
directory holding `.git` -- the same root the repository profile already uses,
so which commands you have follows which repository you are standing in.

## Parsing is separate from reading

`parse` takes text; `discover` takes two functions -- list a directory, read a
file -- and neither touches `node:fs`. So the rules are testable against a map:
what counts as front matter, what a missing name falls back to, what happens to
a file that cannot be parsed.

Three of those rules exist because the obvious implementation is wrong:

- **A value keeps every colon after the first.** A description is a sentence
  and sentences contain URLs. Splitting on all of them cuts the description at
  the first `https:`.
- **An unterminated fence is not front matter.** Treating it as one swallows
  the whole file as headers and leaves the instructions empty.
- **A skill whose front matter has no name is LISTED**, under its directory
  name, carrying a `:problem`. Dropping it would make 「this repository has no
  skills」 and 「I could not read its skill」 the same screen.

## A project command never shadows a built-in

`/status` meaning two things depending on which repository you are standing in
is worse than not having the project one, and a shadow that happens silently is
worse again. Admission runs after every built-in is registered, so the taken
set is complete, and a refusal is a VALUE -- reported under the start screen
with its reason, not dropped.

Refused for three reasons, each named: the name is a built-in, two project
commands claim the same name, or the body is larger than the ceiling (64 KB).
The last one refuses rather than truncating: half a procedure that says nothing
about the other half is worse than a command that says why it is not there.

## What is sent, and what is shown

Invoking `/name args` sends the skill body as instructions and the arguments as
the request, delimited so the agent can tell which is which. No arguments says
so rather than arriving blank.

The transcript shows the LINE THAT WAS TYPED, not the expansion. Echoing ten
kilobytes of instructions where the operator typed one line buries the
conversation in the thing that produced it, so `start-run!` and
`submit-message!` take an echo separate from the text, and the queue keeps both
(`/queue` lists what was typed).

## On the screen

The start screen grew a `Project Commands` section -- three named with their
descriptions and the rest listed by name -- and the counts line gained
`32 project`. `REPL Commands` went back to the built-ins only: thirty-two names
inside it buried the ten an operator uses every session. A repository with no
project commands gets no heading at all, because an empty section reads as a
broken feature rather than an unused one.

`/help` lists all sixty-one, which follows from the registry being the one
decider (ADR-2609062400) and needed no work.

## Measured

Against this workspace: 32 discovered, 0 refused, 61 slash commands in `/help`,
`32 project` on the start screen. `/secrets-location-map <args>` invoked for
real -- the prompt was composed with the body delimited and the arguments
below it, a run started, and ESC stopped it.

Controls, each failing by the name of the check it breaks: letting a project
command shadow a built-in; dropping an unnamed skill instead of listing it;
splitting front matter on every colon.
"}]
